 "in the frame" counts DISTINCT VALUES PRESENT in one 640x480-derived
 level; "reachable" is the alphabet the arithmetic can produce at all, and
 "bits needed" follows the reachable column. The two differ, and the
 difference matters: EXPERIMENTS.md reported 2/5/15/26 for the CV_8U
 ladder, which is what a 256x256 frame CONTAINED -- its level 3 is 32x32,
 i.e. 1024 pixels drawn from an alphabet of 65. A frame statistic falls
 with the frame size; the reachable alphabet does not.

